<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Peters Humphrey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Peters Humphrey was a Canadian legal scholar at McGill University who served as the first director of the Human Rights Division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Secretariat from 1946 to 1966. He prepared the first draft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a>, adopted in 1948, and later helped found Amnesty International <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and other human rights bodies.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Peters Humphrey was born on 30 April 1905 in Hampton, New Brunswick, the son of a shoe merchant. His father died of cancer when he was 13 months old; at six he was badly burned while playing with fire and lost his left arm the following year, and his mother died of cancer in 1916, leaving him an orphan. He entered Mount Allison University at 15, moved to McGill University and took degrees in commerce in 1925, arts in 1927 and law in 1929.</p><p>A fellowship took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he married Jeanne Godreau, whom he had met on the voyage. He practised law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> for five years before becoming a professor at McGill, where he founded the McGill Debating Union. In the early 1940s he met Henri Laugier, a French refugee working for the Free French, who later became Assistant Secretary-General of the United Nations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1946 Laugier appointed Humphrey director of the new Division of Human Rights. After consulting the executive group of the Commission on Human Rights, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-roosevelt">Eleanor Roosevelt</a>, he prepared the first preliminary draft of the Universal Declaration, which after later revisions was adopted unanimously by the General Assembly on 10 December 1948. In twenty years at the UN he oversaw the implementation of 67 international conventions and worked on the freedom of the press, the status of women and racial discrimination. In 1963 he proposed a United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights, an office created more than thirty years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back at McGill from 1966, Humphrey served on the Royal Commission on the Status of Women and with the International League for Human Rights, helped create the Canadian Human Rights Foundation, and took part in inquiries into abuses under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and into the famine of 1932-33 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> on 14 March 1995. He was made an Officer of the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1974 and received the UN Human Rights Prize in 1988, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> unveiled a plaque to him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Peters Humphrey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…build post-war institutions: Lester B. Pearson won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1957 for proposing the United Nations Emergency Force during the Suez Crisis, which set the pattern for armed peacekeeping, and the lawyer <b>John Peters Humphrey</b> prepared the first draft of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hampton, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.53,-65.83&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Hampton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2989 558 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Peters Humphrey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3439 828h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3439 828h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.53,-65.83&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
